MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:10.980
Ian, you mentioned CUDA.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:11.950
That's a thing.

00:06:11.950 --> 00:06:13.350
Do you want to tell us a little
bit of the history of that,

00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:16.200
or like just a broad overview of
what that is and what that does

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:17.425
and like all those things?

00:06:17.425 --> 00:06:18.300
We have half an hour.

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:18.850
It's fine.

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.030
IAN BUCK: Yeah, no sure.

00:06:20.030 --> 00:06:21.230
It's a kind of a fun story.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:24.300
Back in around 2000, obviously
the video game industry

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:28.050
was driving a ton
of investment in PCs

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.342
and graphics accelerators
for playing video games.

00:06:30.342 --> 00:06:31.800
As a result, those
video games were

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.100
getting more and more realistic
and more and more programmable.

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.022
Bunch of academics,
myself included,

00:06:37.022 --> 00:06:38.730
realizing that maybe
these browsers could

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.410
be used for something
other than playing "Quake"

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:41.460
and "Doom" in the evening.

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:42.585
SPEAKER 1: I'm pinned down.

00:06:42.585 --> 00:06:43.560
[EXPLOSION]

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:44.550
IAN BUCK: Which, of
course, we all were--

00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:44.820
MARK MANDEL: Which is fine.

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:45.893
IAN BUCK: --we all
were doing that, too.

00:06:45.893 --> 00:06:46.366
MARK MANDEL: Which is fine.

00:06:46.366 --> 00:06:47.650
KARI BRISKI: Not that there's
anything wrong with that.

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.080
IAN BUCK: So we started
looking at these clusters.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.675
And it turns out they were
like doubling in performance,

00:06:52.675 --> 00:06:54.300
like how many floating
point operations

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.160
they can do,
literally every year

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:57.900
and repeatedly year over year.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:06:59.652
And they were pretty
hard to program, just

00:06:59.652 --> 00:07:01.110
from a general
purpose perspective,

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:03.840
using graphics APIs
like OpenGL and DX.

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.420
So I started a project
early on at Stanford

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.180
called Brook, which was to
explore how can we program

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.040
these things in a more
general purpose way,

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.380
in a way that exploited
their sort of natural ability

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:16.260
to double in performance,
to defy Moore's law,

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.350
but be usable by a general
programming audience in C

00:07:19.350 --> 00:07:20.352
and other languages.

00:07:20.352 --> 00:07:21.810
That was a very
successful project,

00:07:21.810 --> 00:07:24.930
open sourced it, got it out
there, got over 10,000 users.

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.540
And then Jensen, our CEO,
invited me to come to NVIDIA

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:29.010
and do it for real.

00:07:29.010 --> 00:07:32.580
So we started CUDA back in
2004, learned all those lessons

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:34.408
of how to do
parallel programming

00:07:34.408 --> 00:07:36.825
kind of for the masses on a
different kind of accelerator,

00:07:36.825 --> 00:07:39.300
a massively parallel one, a GPU.

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:41.670
And took it off from
there, launched in 2006,

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.070
and it instantly
became very successful

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:46.260
in high performance
computing and supercomputing,

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:47.298
and today in AI.

00:07:47.298 --> 00:07:48.090
JON FOUST: Awesome.

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.340
On behalf of many millions
of gamers around the world,

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:51.038
thank you.

00:07:51.038 --> 00:07:52.230
IAN BUCK: You're welcome.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:54.480
JON FOUST: And I'm really
excited to see the big shift

00:07:54.480 --> 00:07:57.342
towards general computing and
processing, so really excited.

00:07:57.342 --> 00:07:58.800
IAN BUCK: What
makes it so exciting

00:07:58.800 --> 00:08:00.050
is that it's so accessible.

00:08:00.050 --> 00:08:03.300
You know, anyone can go
to Fry's, buy a laptop

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:06.200
with a GPU in it and get
access to the platform,

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:09.795
download it for free, and even
on their gaming PC, you know,

00:08:09.795 --> 00:08:11.670
try it out and figure
out what they could do.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:14.520
We've heard all about these
stories of PhD students

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:16.260
or graduate students
or even undergrads,

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:18.990
you know, blowing away
their peers or professors

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.460
or classmates on kind of the
stuff they can do with GPUs

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:21.960
that--

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:23.520
it was never even
imaginable when

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:24.935
we started this whole thing.

00:08:24.935 --> 00:08:26.810
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, Kari,
I think probably you

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:28.685
have a good perspective
on like, where do you

00:08:28.685 --> 00:08:31.100
see CUDA and the things that
people are doing with GPUs,

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.440
what are the applications people
are doing with that right now?

00:08:33.440 --> 00:08:35.120
KARI BRISKI: Oh, yeah,
like Ian said, there's

00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:36.169
a variety of application.

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:39.230
But with our focus on AI, what's
kind of exciting and happening

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:42.710
is, the easy thing
to say is everywhere.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:45.385
But if I were to sort of
break down AI applications

00:08:45.385 --> 00:08:46.760
and what our
customers are doing,

00:08:46.760 --> 00:08:48.950
I sort of bucket them
into three groups.

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:52.460
And that's like computer vision,
speech and audio, and then

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:54.200
machine comprehension
and [? OP. ?]

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.943
And so you can even break
those down into deeper

00:08:56.943 --> 00:08:59.360
lower buckets of different
types of neural networks, which

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:00.740
we call the Cambrian Explosion.

00:09:00.740 --> 00:09:03.710
Because almost every
task under these buckets

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:06.050
can be applied with
a neural network.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.430
IAN BUCK: It's awesome to
see things evolve over time.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:11.590
It all started with
that first AlexNet.

00:09:11.590 --> 00:09:13.650
Alex Krizhevsky, a
guy up in Canada,

00:09:13.650 --> 00:09:16.040
he was working on this
thing, which had been totally

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.720
forgotten about,
deep neural networks,

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.460
as a researcher
working with Hinton.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:21.650
And you know, heard
about this thing called

00:09:21.650 --> 00:09:23.190
CUDA, heard about
this stuff, math

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.130
that we were doing
with HPC, and realized

00:09:25.130 --> 00:09:27.950
it was the same math that
he was doing in his research

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:31.640
in AI, which that back then
was still the AI winter.

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:34.460
He wrote the first
accelerated framework for AI

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:35.690
and invented AlexNet.

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:36.982
That's why it's called AlexNet.

00:09:36.982 --> 00:09:40.340
It's named after And he did it
on a bunch of gaming GPUs, two

00:09:40.340 --> 00:09:42.830
GTX 570s way back in--

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:44.120
I guess it was 2012.

00:09:44.120 --> 00:09:46.380
And it all took off from there.

00:09:46.380 --> 00:09:49.267
Today, I think we have
a massive collection

00:09:49.267 --> 00:09:50.850
of different kinds
of neural networks,

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:52.370
different applications of AI.

00:09:52.370 --> 00:09:53.703
And that's what makes it so fun.

00:09:53.703 --> 00:09:55.786
This whole field is
constantly reinventing itself.

00:09:55.786 --> 00:09:57.286
KARI BRISKI: Which
I think is funny,

00:09:57.286 --> 00:09:58.600
he said, way back in 2012.

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:00.040
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:00.670
And it's--

00:10:00.670 --> 00:10:01.120
MARK MANDEL: It's not
that long ago, right?

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:02.150
KARI BRISKI: It's
not that long ago,

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:04.550
and it's actually-- you know,
AlexNet's considered like a toy

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:06.633
now, considering the new
neural networks which are

00:10:06.633 --> 00:10:09.065
really deep and very complex.

00:10:09.065 --> 00:10:12.758
JON FOUST: I actually still
have my GTX 680, I think.

00:10:12.758 --> 00:10:13.550
IAN BUCK: Oh, yeah.

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:14.990
JON FOUST: It's somewhere
collecting dust.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:16.580
I'll probably end
up using AlexNet

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:18.828
and see if I can mess
around and do some things.

00:10:18.828 --> 00:10:20.870
IAN BUCK: Yeah, we've made
things a little faster

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:21.945
since then.

00:10:21.945 --> 00:10:23.070
MARK MANDEL: Just a little.

00:10:23.070 --> 00:10:24.487
IAN BUCK: Those
first GPUs, again,

00:10:24.487 --> 00:10:27.480
were designed for HPC computing
use cases and, of course,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:28.340
video games.

00:10:28.340 --> 00:10:31.280
But since then, we've been
constantly reinventing our GPU.

00:10:31.280 --> 00:10:33.770
In fact, we pump through and
create new architectures,

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:35.990
new software stacks
literally every year

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.810
because that's how fast things
are changing in this world.

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:40.310
MARK MANDEL: Speaking
of new things,

00:10:40.310 --> 00:10:42.950
I know you'll introduce
something new recently,

00:10:42.950 --> 00:10:43.908
maybe something cool?

00:10:43.908 --> 00:10:45.200
JON FOUST: Super awesome, yeah.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.570
IAN BUCK: We just came out
of our GTC conference, which

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:50.090
is our main developer
conference, which we invite

00:10:50.090 --> 00:10:52.130
the world to come and
we share some of things

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:52.790
that we've been working on.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:53.630
They share the
stuff that they've

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:55.760
been doing on our platform,
which is really exciting.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:57.802
We've been improving our
AI performance for sure.

00:10:57.802 --> 00:10:58.910
There's two parts of AI.

00:10:58.910 --> 00:11:01.535
There's the training part, where
we're teaching neural networks

00:11:01.535 --> 00:11:02.368
to learn new things.

00:11:02.368 --> 00:11:03.993
One of the things
that we've been doing

00:11:03.993 --> 00:11:05.780
is actually making
it faster and easier

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.450
to get the peak performance
out of our GPUs.

00:11:08.450 --> 00:11:10.490
And particularly in
frameworks like TensorFlow--

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:12.590
courtesy of you guys, Google,
which we work very closely

00:11:12.590 --> 00:11:13.220
with--

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:15.440
getting access to
the performance that

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:17.450
are available in our
latest GPU core--

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:20.480
it's called Tensor Core, which
is a mixed-precision core that

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:23.605
can do things in 64-bit floating
point, 32-bit floating point,

00:11:23.605 --> 00:11:25.970
16-bit, even 8-bit
because AI can

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:28.010
take advantage of all
those different precisions

00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:30.380
and the different
layers of performance.

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:32.840
The new thing we announced
was TensorFlow AMP,

00:11:32.840 --> 00:11:35.480
which is our NVIDIA
AMP acceleration

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:37.045
into the TensorFlow
framework to take

00:11:37.045 --> 00:11:38.420
advantage of that
mixed precision

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:39.950
automatically for users.

00:11:39.950 --> 00:11:42.280
It will automatically
pick the right precision

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:44.030
for the right layers
of the neural network

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:45.860
and maximize performance.

00:11:45.860 --> 00:11:48.940
We're seeing huge speed-ups
This is like 2x, 3x even

00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:50.040
for AI training.

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:52.130
And it's literally a
one to two-line change

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:53.360
in your TensorFlow script.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:54.980
KARI BRISKI: Yeah, the goal was
to give a great experience out

00:11:54.980 --> 00:11:56.900
of the box, so that the deep
learning practitioner doesn't

00:11:56.900 --> 00:11:58.482
have to change the hardware.

00:11:58.482 --> 00:12:00.440
They don't have to change
their hyperparameters

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:02.982
working in the framework that
you feel most comfortable with,

00:12:02.982 --> 00:12:04.880
which, for a lot of
people, is TensorFlow.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.390
We also have some solutions
for PyTorch and MXNet as well.

00:12:08.390 --> 00:12:10.550
But AMP stands for
Automatic Mixed Precision,

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:14.210
which is that training technique
for being able to feature input

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:17.327
in FP16 and accumulate
FP32, which turns out

00:12:17.327 --> 00:12:19.910
is really important for keeping
the accuracy of your gradients

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:20.645
through training.

00:12:20.645 --> 00:12:22.520
MARK MANDEL: You said
a bunch of words there.

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:24.053
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:24.053 --> 00:12:25.970
Some of them, I might
even know what they are.

00:12:25.970 --> 00:12:27.650
Can you break that
down a little further?

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:29.442
KARI BRISKI: Yeah, I
think if I have, like,

00:12:29.442 --> 00:12:32.360
three takeaways that I want you
to take away for AMP, Automatic

00:12:32.360 --> 00:12:33.905
Mixed Precision,
is that you only

00:12:33.905 --> 00:12:36.530
have to change two lines of code
and things just go way faster.

00:12:36.530 --> 00:12:36.990
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:38.480
KARI BRISKI: So if you're a
deep learning practitioner

00:12:38.480 --> 00:12:40.522
or you have training
[? on any ?] neural networks

00:12:40.522 --> 00:12:42.467
and maybe it takes,
like, a day to train,

00:12:42.467 --> 00:12:43.800
you can now do it in half a day.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:45.980
So then you can run double the
experiments right out the box

00:12:45.980 --> 00:12:47.405
without doing
anything different,

00:12:47.405 --> 00:12:49.050
so that's kind of cool.

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:51.702
And then there is no accuracy
versus speed trade-off

00:12:51.702 --> 00:12:52.910
for mixed-precision training.

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:54.810
And I want to be pretty
clear about that.

00:12:54.810 --> 00:12:56.240
Because you say lower precision.

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:58.100
And yeah, you're
training in less bits--

00:12:58.100 --> 00:12:59.990
FP16 instead of FP32.

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:02.330
But you're not losing
accuracy, and so I

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:04.310
want to be very clear
about that and have

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:05.585
people understand that.

00:13:05.585 --> 00:13:08.210
And that's the third takeaway is
that Automatic Mixed Precision

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:10.550
training is general
purpose across a variety

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:12.020
of different applications.

00:13:12.020 --> 00:13:14.645
IAN BUCK: Yeah, there's a lot of
talk about precision and using

00:13:14.645 --> 00:13:17.750
all sorts of different
numerical formats and math.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:19.850
It's really hard to get
the peak performance

00:13:19.850 --> 00:13:21.950
and not do any compromises.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.290
We've made that easier by
putting all that smarts,

00:13:24.290 --> 00:13:27.600
all that intelligent, all those
numerics and complex equations

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:29.280
and loss functions--
more words--

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:31.350
[LAUGHTER]

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:32.500
--into a box for you.

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:34.680
And that was one of the
big engagements in talking

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:35.910
with our customers
is that they want

00:13:35.910 --> 00:13:38.010
to get the best performance,
but they're not the ninja data

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:38.670
scientists.

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.650
They just want to get the best
performance out of the GPUs

00:13:40.650 --> 00:13:41.310
that they're buying.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:43.035
And we've made it a
ton easier with AMP,

00:13:43.035 --> 00:13:44.160
and we're excited about it.

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.370
JON FOUST: So GCP and NVIDIA--

00:13:46.370 --> 00:13:48.370
what kind of hardware are
we launching together?

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:49.995
IAN BUCK: GCP has
been a great partner.

00:13:49.995 --> 00:13:52.360
You guys always want to put
the best GPUs in the cloud--

00:13:52.360 --> 00:13:52.670
MARK MANDEL: We try.

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:53.962
IAN BUCK: --for your customers.

00:13:53.962 --> 00:13:56.340
And you have since
the days of Kepler,

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:57.820
which has been fantastic.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:02.100
We now have our Volta V100 GPUs
in the cloud with TensorCore.

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.950
AMP is specifically designed
to run those lightning fast.

00:14:04.950 --> 00:14:09.638
And then just last year, we also
announced our T4 GPUs with GCP.

00:14:09.638 --> 00:14:11.430
This is a little bit
different kind of GPU.

00:14:11.430 --> 00:14:14.340
It's designed for inference,
the other side of AI.

00:14:14.340 --> 00:14:16.150
One is training,
teaching a neural network

00:14:16.150 --> 00:14:17.580
how to recognize things--

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:20.340
speech, the words I'm saying,
what's inside of an image.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:21.510
That's the learning part.

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:23.610
Then there's the deployment
part, the scale-out,

00:14:23.610 --> 00:14:26.340
the running of the services,
the deployment of AI.

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:29.310
In the past, that used to
run on whatever CPUs you had.

00:14:29.310 --> 00:14:31.380
And typically, there
was a compromise.

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:33.000
You picked a small
neural network,

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:35.010
or maybe you couldn't
do everything.

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:38.355
The new AI that's coming out
now are incredibly intelligent,

00:14:38.355 --> 00:14:39.480
very powerful, impressive--

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:40.630
KARI BRISKI: Complex.

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:42.450
IAN BUCK: They're
also very big--

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.040
lots of floating
point operations.

00:14:44.040 --> 00:14:45.473
And this has to be real-time.

00:14:45.473 --> 00:14:46.890
It's got to recognize
my speech as

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:49.307
fast as I'm talking right now,
which is probably too fast.

00:14:49.307 --> 00:14:50.348
MARK MANDEL: You're fine.

00:14:50.348 --> 00:14:50.870
You're good.

00:14:50.870 --> 00:14:52.620
IAN BUCK: That's why
people are now moving

00:14:52.620 --> 00:14:54.610
inference to acceleration.

00:14:54.610 --> 00:14:56.220
So you guys were
the first, actually,

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:58.320
to launch T4 in the
cloud, congratulations.

00:14:58.320 --> 00:15:01.440
And we've been working together
to make inference even faster

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:02.990
jointly with customers.

00:15:02.990 --> 00:15:04.560
KARI BRISKI: Yeah, and just
for the audience out there,

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:06.143
inference is taking
that trained model

00:15:06.143 --> 00:15:07.380
and using it for predictions.

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:09.297
I think a lot people use
the term predictions,

00:15:09.297 --> 00:15:10.950
and we use the term inference.

00:15:10.950 --> 00:15:12.060
MARK MANDEL: What kind
of speed increases

00:15:12.060 --> 00:15:13.080
are we talking about here?

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:14.330
IAN BUCK: Orders of magnitude.

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:16.440
I mean, some stuff-- it
was not possible before.

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:20.100
Processing audio, doing
intelligent decision-making--

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:22.710
If I ask a question, I want
to have an intelligent answer

00:15:22.710 --> 00:15:24.780
back, not just what
Wikipedia says--

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:27.037
or actually be able
to produce speech--

00:15:27.037 --> 00:15:28.620
I don't think people
realize that when

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:29.787
computers talk back to you--

00:15:29.787 --> 00:15:31.703
you may haven't noticed
in the last few years,

00:15:31.703 --> 00:15:33.030
it's gotten really good.

00:15:33.030 --> 00:15:35.722
It's really good at
sounding like a human.

00:15:35.722 --> 00:15:37.680
That's actually because
of a new neural network

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:39.390
technology called WaveNets.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:40.950
WaveNets actually
take in basically

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.260
a robotic-sounding
voice and output

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:44.910
a nice sounding
voice like you and I.

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:47.035
JON FOUST: Oh, I think I've
actually heard of that.

00:15:47.035 --> 00:15:49.680
It's like you read off
roughly around 200 lines.

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.645
And it starts to
imitate your own voice

00:15:51.645 --> 00:15:52.770
so it can read back to you.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:54.230
IAN BUCK: And this
is exactly how

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:56.490
text-to-speech services work.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.210
And they have a lot
of applications--

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:00.810
not just the home
speaker device,

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.180
but even in businesses,
in call centers,

00:16:03.180 --> 00:16:06.210
and how we talk to computers
through automation systems.

00:16:06.210 --> 00:16:08.580
If they sound natural, people
want to engage with them.

00:16:08.580 --> 00:16:11.038
And it goes right to the bottom
line of an engagement score

00:16:11.038 --> 00:16:13.090
and a SAT score for a business.

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:15.000
So this is one of the
new hot areas in AI.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:17.910
It's one of the big demand
generators for our GPUs.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.460
Because you just can't run
WaveNets on legacy platforms.

00:16:20.460 --> 00:16:22.320
They're too big and
too complicated.

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:24.090
And they're too important
not to get right

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:25.530
for those kinds of services.

00:16:25.530 --> 00:16:27.330
KARI BRISKI: Yeah, and you asked
exactly what kind of speed-ups,

00:16:27.330 --> 00:16:28.980
and it does depend on the model.

00:16:28.980 --> 00:16:31.430
And we call it finding
your quality of service.

00:16:31.430 --> 00:16:35.040
So if you have these ensemble
models or an output of one

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:37.147
model feeds into the
input of another model,

00:16:37.147 --> 00:16:38.730
and they all have
to be done in, like,

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.350
this enormous cosmic power
in itty-bitty living space

00:16:41.350 --> 00:16:42.097
of latency--

00:16:42.097 --> 00:16:43.680
and so if you add
all that latency up,

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:46.180
it has to have that delightful
end-user experience.

00:16:46.180 --> 00:16:48.460
So if you speak into a
search query and then

00:16:48.460 --> 00:16:50.930
it text-to-speech back to
you, synthesizing speech back

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:53.430
to you, that all has to happen
in a nice little tiny window.

00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:54.340
MARK MANDEL: Tiny windows.

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:55.770
IAN BUCK: Yeah, so
latency matters.

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:57.145
If I'm going to
ask a question, I

00:16:57.145 --> 00:16:59.242
can't wait a second for
an answer to come back.

00:16:59.242 --> 00:17:00.450
I just won't use the service.

00:17:00.450 --> 00:17:02.158
I won't use it on my
phone, the consumer.

00:17:02.158 --> 00:17:05.230
So you have to deliver responses
in a tenth of a second.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:07.950
Otherwise, after that,
the enjoyment score

00:17:07.950 --> 00:17:09.869
starts to fall off and
people stop using it.

00:17:09.869 --> 00:17:12.780
So we are seeing
throughputs and speed-ups

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:16.650
in the 10x to 100x by
moving onto GPUs, especially

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:19.109
for some of the new
models like WaveNets

00:17:19.109 --> 00:17:21.927
for natural language processing,
like the new model called BERT.

00:17:21.927 --> 00:17:23.010
MARK MANDEL: What is this?

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:23.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:23.550 --> 00:17:26.258
IAN BUCK: Yeah, these guys
come up with great names.

00:17:26.258 --> 00:17:28.050
I encourage anyone
listening who's actually

00:17:28.050 --> 00:17:29.330
working on data science--

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:29.790
KARI BRISKI: An Ernie?

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:30.873
IAN BUCK: We need a Ernie.

00:17:30.873 --> 00:17:31.662
We've got a Bert.

00:17:31.662 --> 00:17:32.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:33.650
So BERT stands for--

00:17:33.650 --> 00:17:34.487
Kari, can you do it?

00:17:34.487 --> 00:17:36.570
KARI BRISKI: Bidirectional
Encoder Representations

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:37.680
in Transformers.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.360
[SCIENCE FICTION SOUND]

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:42.420
MARK MANDEL: Neat,
what does that mean?

00:17:42.420 --> 00:17:44.400
IAN BUCK: BERT is the
new neural network

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:47.100
that's used to understand
language, language being what

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:48.690
we call natural
language processing.

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:50.250
You give it a web page.

00:17:50.250 --> 00:17:52.980
Please summarize that web
page-- understanding text

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:54.720
and contents and languages.

00:17:54.720 --> 00:17:56.670
As I'm speaking,
understanding what I mean,

00:17:56.670 --> 00:17:58.540
and what's the sentiment,
and summarize it.

00:17:58.540 --> 00:18:00.900
This is very important for
things like advertising,

00:18:00.900 --> 00:18:02.010
for search.

00:18:02.010 --> 00:18:05.490
I need to know, is this tweet
a good tweet, a bad tweet?

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:06.900
Who should I share it with?

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:10.230
Is it positive toward
so-and-so or negative?

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:12.030
It's the new model
that has come up--

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:15.787
it's been talked about as the
grand unifying natural language

00:18:15.787 --> 00:18:17.870
processing model that can
understand all language.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:20.320
They have many different
models for different use cases.

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:21.480
This is the latest one.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:22.530
KARI BRISKI: It's a
great multitasker.

00:18:22.530 --> 00:18:23.610
IAN BUCK: It's huge.

00:18:23.610 --> 00:18:24.577
It's gigantic.

00:18:24.577 --> 00:18:26.410
But it's very intelligent
and very powerful.

00:18:26.410 --> 00:18:29.050
It's being used already in
production today with certain

00:18:29.050 --> 00:18:31.420
companies for doing things
like sentiment analysis,

00:18:31.420 --> 00:18:33.580
content filtering-- is
this a safe thing to share,

00:18:33.580 --> 00:18:35.000
not a safe thing to share--

00:18:35.000 --> 00:18:38.230
a very important part of AI
today and one of the driving

00:18:38.230 --> 00:18:40.430
forces for moving AI to GPUs.

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:42.430
JON FOUST: Our listeners
obviously can't see me,

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.317
but I have this huge
grin on my face.

00:18:44.317 --> 00:18:46.150
So many light bulbs are
going off in my mind

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:47.440
right now for future projects.

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:49.450
And this sounds
completely awesome.

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:52.242
So curious--
availability for the T4?

00:18:52.242 --> 00:18:53.950
IAN BUCK: It's available
today, so people

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:55.600
can rent it right now from GCP.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:57.035
So if you're an
on-prem customer,

00:18:57.035 --> 00:18:58.660
you get it-- also
available on servers.

00:18:58.660 --> 00:19:00.040
We're available everywhere.

00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:02.680
Our mission is to
make our technology

00:19:02.680 --> 00:19:04.360
available through every channel.

00:19:04.360 --> 00:19:07.743
The cloud is a great time
to market, especially GCP.

00:19:07.743 --> 00:19:09.160
So we're excited
that you guys are

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:12.110
leaning in and getting our
latest technology out there.

00:19:12.110 --> 00:19:13.730
We also make our
software available.

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:16.210
So we do a huge amount of
work, and people don't always

00:19:16.210 --> 00:19:18.830
understand this, to
accelerate the frameworks--

00:19:18.830 --> 00:19:20.578
the AI frameworks
like TensorFlow--

00:19:20.578 --> 00:19:22.120
or the rest of the
inference software

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:24.040
stacks, so that
users of the cloud

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:25.930
can very quickly
deploy these models

00:19:25.930 --> 00:19:28.150
or build services on top of it.

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:32.320
We publish optimized framework
containers about every month

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:33.730
now across the board--

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:36.550
TensorFlow but also PyTorch
and MXNet and the other guys.

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:38.980
We also publish a bunch of
our inference software, which

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:41.980
Kari can explain more, to allow
customers to just not have

00:19:41.980 --> 00:19:44.890
to build AI from scratch but
start from standard building

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:45.400
blocks.

00:19:45.400 --> 00:19:48.760
And all that runs on GCP
right out of the gate.

00:19:48.760 --> 00:19:50.260
KARI BRISKI: Yeah,
so when Ian-- you

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:53.360
said that T4 has a little bit
of everything for everyone.

00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:55.690
The FP64, it's got FP32.

00:19:55.690 --> 00:19:57.010
You get FP16.

00:19:57.010 --> 00:19:57.890
You get [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:57.890 --> 00:20:00.490
But our software is built
in so that you can actually

00:20:00.490 --> 00:20:03.820
flip a switch and exploit
these features like Tensor

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:05.320
Cores inside the hardware.

00:20:05.320 --> 00:20:09.410
And I want to say the FP16 is
a great format for inference.

00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:11.620
I mentioned earlier that
we had this mixed precision

00:20:11.620 --> 00:20:13.120
for training, but
you don't actually

00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.745
have to have that
for inference either.

00:20:14.745 --> 00:20:16.490
You can literally
just flip a switch

00:20:16.490 --> 00:20:18.940
and know that you'll get
the same sort of accuracy.

00:20:18.940 --> 00:20:21.310
With INT8, there is a little
bit more consideration

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.710
that has to be taken in
both with your training

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:26.080
and quantization of these
weights and activations.

00:20:26.080 --> 00:20:29.308
But you do get benefits
of memory space and power

00:20:29.308 --> 00:20:30.850
limitations when
you actually take it

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.282
to this reduced precision.

00:20:33.282 --> 00:20:35.240
And we offer software
that can do that for you.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:37.407
And one thing that we did
is this software framework

00:20:37.407 --> 00:20:40.470
called TensorRT, and we've
integrated it into TensorFlow.

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:42.786
So it's a lot of
tensors going on.

00:20:42.786 --> 00:20:43.422
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:43.422 --> 00:20:45.130
MARK MANDEL: It's all
right, you had GTC.

00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:45.880
We're GCP.

00:20:45.880 --> 00:20:46.473
It's all fine.

00:20:46.473 --> 00:20:46.973
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:46.973 --> 00:20:48.580
KARI BRISKI: Yeah, exactly.

00:20:48.580 --> 00:20:50.800
So what we've done is we've
taken that software that

00:20:50.800 --> 00:20:53.320
takes the best advantage
of optimizations

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:55.060
and put it right
into where people

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:57.640
do most of their work,
which is TensorFlow.

00:20:57.640 --> 00:20:59.860
IAN BUCK: And then we
push all this software

00:20:59.860 --> 00:21:02.590
on the web free to
download as containers.

00:21:02.590 --> 00:21:03.600
They can pull from--

00:21:03.600 --> 00:21:04.750
MARK MANDEL: I assume
like Docker containers,

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:05.680
and it's all standard?

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:06.400
IAN BUCK: It's all standard.

00:21:06.400 --> 00:21:07.390
It's all in Docker containers.

00:21:07.390 --> 00:21:09.830
And in fact, we also have
examples of all the models.

00:21:09.830 --> 00:21:12.043
So they don't need to
download separate models

00:21:12.043 --> 00:21:12.710
from everywhere.

00:21:12.710 --> 00:21:14.877
It's all captured in the
containers as a straight up

00:21:14.877 --> 00:21:17.443
building block to fire up and
run and create a service built

00:21:17.443 --> 00:21:18.110
on these things.

00:21:18.110 --> 00:21:21.100
So if you want the latest BERT
that's been optimized for GPUs

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:24.040
or the latest ResNet 50
or whatever have you,

00:21:24.040 --> 00:21:25.450
feel free to come to NVIDIA.com.

00:21:25.450 --> 00:21:28.458
They're on-- it's
actually NGC.NVIDIA.com.

00:21:28.458 --> 00:21:30.250
You can download all
those bits and pieces.

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:33.400
They all are qualified
and run on GCP.

00:21:33.400 --> 00:21:35.410
We even have some
HPC stuff on there.

00:21:35.410 --> 00:21:36.830
There's some base
CUDA containers,

00:21:36.830 --> 00:21:39.163
if you want to actually get
your hands dirty and program

00:21:39.163 --> 00:21:40.150
GPUs directly.

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:41.860
All that software
is freely available

00:21:41.860 --> 00:21:44.790
and maintained and ready
to run on GCP's cloud.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:46.240
Again, the URL is--

00:21:46.240 --> 00:21:49.460
MARK MANDEL: We'll put a link in
the show notes, make that easy.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:51.850
Saying URLs out loud is
complicated and hard.

00:21:51.850 --> 00:21:52.697
KARI BRISKI: Yes.

00:21:52.697 --> 00:21:55.030
IAN BUCK: You know, we're
seeing that shift from on-prem

00:21:55.030 --> 00:21:56.488
to the cloud
conversation happening

00:21:56.488 --> 00:21:59.650
in all sorts of places, not
just obviously in the AI world,

00:21:59.650 --> 00:22:01.630
but also in the traditional
computing space.

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:03.200
The high-performance
computing guys,

00:22:03.200 --> 00:22:07.450
the people doing simulations for
drug discovery or understanding

00:22:07.450 --> 00:22:09.970
astrophysics and how
galaxies and stars--

00:22:09.970 --> 00:22:11.260
we do a lot of that work too.

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:13.690
And actually, all
that can be done also

00:22:13.690 --> 00:22:14.980
on the same GPUs in the cloud.

00:22:14.980 --> 00:22:16.810
So some of the
research community,

00:22:16.810 --> 00:22:20.082
the higher-ed guys, people
doing science and discovery

00:22:20.082 --> 00:22:21.790
can take advantage of
these GPUs as well.

00:22:21.790 --> 00:22:22.540
JON FOUST: That's awesome.

00:22:22.540 --> 00:22:23.230
MARK MANDEL: No, that's
very interesting.

00:22:23.230 --> 00:22:24.330
And now I'm actually
kind of curious.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:25.770
You brought the
on-prem discussion in,

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:26.980
which is always interesting.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:29.830
Where do you see people using
the cloud versus on-prem

00:22:29.830 --> 00:22:31.030
and the trade-offs therein?

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:33.113
And what helps people make
those sort of decisions

00:22:33.113 --> 00:22:35.290
for using GPUs on
servers, essentially?

00:22:35.290 --> 00:22:37.028
IAN BUCK: A lot of
this new AI technology

00:22:37.028 --> 00:22:38.320
is being invented in the cloud.

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:39.695
That's what makes
it so exciting.

00:22:39.695 --> 00:22:42.160
And what I love about the
cloud is the accessibility.

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:45.280
You just can get access right
away and to try it, to use it.

00:22:45.280 --> 00:22:47.740
It's a few bucks an hour
or even cents an hour

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:52.780
to get access to a GPU and
start using GPUs for AI or CUDA

00:22:52.780 --> 00:22:53.590
or whatever.

00:22:53.590 --> 00:22:55.270
You don't have to
go through the IT.

00:22:55.270 --> 00:22:57.268
We do see trade-offs
at the larger scale.

00:22:57.268 --> 00:22:58.810
Business decisions--
what makes sense

00:22:58.810 --> 00:23:01.210
to keep on-prem versus
push in the cloud?

00:23:01.210 --> 00:23:03.760
Some of that's economics for
sure, but others, you know,

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:05.500
security or whether
or not they just

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:08.382
want to maintain that
as their own enclave

00:23:08.382 --> 00:23:10.090
that they can control
because it's really

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:11.382
important for their businesses.

00:23:11.382 --> 00:23:13.120
From our standpoint,
we're agnostic.

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:15.370
My job is to make our
technology available through all

00:23:15.370 --> 00:23:16.450
the channels.

00:23:16.450 --> 00:23:18.255
And I certainly like
working with GCP

00:23:18.255 --> 00:23:19.630
because I know
how aggressive you

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:22.310
guys are at bringing the
latest technology to the cloud.

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:23.935
KARI BRISKI: Yeah,
one of the big deals

00:23:23.935 --> 00:23:25.970
for on-prem versus cloud
and some of the work

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:28.310
that we've done is with
the Kubernetes community,

00:23:28.310 --> 00:23:30.710
so making sure that GPUs
were first-class citizens

00:23:30.710 --> 00:23:32.450
and something that's
completely vanilla to the work

00:23:32.450 --> 00:23:33.200
that you're doing.

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:35.810
So you can have your Kubernetes
cluster deployment on-prem,

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:38.780
and then you can burst to the
cloud with the same settings.

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:40.670
We've done a lot of
work there with being

00:23:40.670 --> 00:23:43.190
able to make sure that
it's pumping out metrics

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:46.420
of GPU utilization,
power metrics,

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:49.307
you know, how are you--
that system administrator,

00:23:49.307 --> 00:23:51.890
what is the health of my system
and how do GPUs fit into that?

00:23:51.890 --> 00:23:54.090
We want to make it as
vanilla as possible.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:56.765
We don't want GPUs to be viewed
as this unique and beautiful

00:23:56.765 --> 00:23:57.890
snowflake-- which they are.

00:23:57.890 --> 00:23:59.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:59.030 --> 00:24:00.785
MARK MANDEL: Of
course, they're lovely.

00:24:00.785 --> 00:24:01.660
Don't get them wrong.

00:24:01.660 --> 00:24:03.710
KARI BRISKI: I did want to bring
up a little bit the inference

00:24:03.710 --> 00:24:04.430
server.

00:24:04.430 --> 00:24:07.190
You kind of have these three
basic blocks when you're

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:08.690
working through AI deployments.

00:24:08.690 --> 00:24:10.877
You have the
preprocessing of the data,

00:24:10.877 --> 00:24:13.460
you have the training, and then
maybe if you're really expert,

00:24:13.460 --> 00:24:15.570
you have this optimization
and then deployment.

00:24:15.570 --> 00:24:18.440
And I hate to use something as
mundane as DevOps for something

00:24:18.440 --> 00:24:21.320
as exciting as AI, but
that's kind of what it is.

00:24:21.320 --> 00:24:23.560
And I think some people
who might get scared about,

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:26.240
where do I begin with
AI model development--

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:28.740
you just take that same
practice and approach to it.

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:31.440
And we have tools at every step
along the way for every person,

00:24:31.440 --> 00:24:33.590
whether you are
data preprocessing,

00:24:33.590 --> 00:24:36.530
if you're a deep learning
data scientist and researcher,

00:24:36.530 --> 00:24:39.080
if you're the software developer
who is trying to integrate

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:41.018
the model into that
application, and then

00:24:41.018 --> 00:24:42.560
the system administrator
who's trying

00:24:42.560 --> 00:24:44.267
to take care of all
this stuff and keep

00:24:44.267 --> 00:24:45.600
the health of their environment.

00:24:45.600 --> 00:24:47.100
IAN BUCK: Yeah, so
you can see we're

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:49.490
pretty active at all layers
of that software stack,

00:24:49.490 --> 00:24:52.430
not just the cutting edge of
the actual development, new AI

00:24:52.430 --> 00:24:54.017
usage, but all the
way down the system

00:24:54.017 --> 00:24:55.100
management and monitoring.

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:57.592
And like Kari said, all
the excitement around

00:24:57.592 --> 00:25:00.050
Kubernetes-- we're definitely
in there working very closely

00:25:00.050 --> 00:25:03.242
with that community to make
sure that it's all GPU-ready.

00:25:03.242 --> 00:25:04.700
The other area
that's hot right now

00:25:04.700 --> 00:25:06.660
is the traditional
machine learning.

00:25:06.660 --> 00:25:09.500
We talk a lot about AI,
but a lot of that math--

00:25:09.500 --> 00:25:11.798
it's reinvigorating
data science.

00:25:11.798 --> 00:25:13.340
There's a lot of
algorithms out there

00:25:13.340 --> 00:25:17.030
that actually are well proven
and very successful for doing

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:20.210
predictive analytics
that are with more

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:23.780
traditional linear
regressions, k-mean clustering,

00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:25.548
gradient boosting
decision trees.

00:25:25.548 --> 00:25:27.590
We've also started to
accelerate those workloads.

00:25:27.590 --> 00:25:29.480
It's the similar kind
of math and operations

00:25:29.480 --> 00:25:30.620
that we were doing before.

00:25:30.620 --> 00:25:34.280
We've integrated with Google on
some of your Cloud ML pipeline

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:37.422
workflows to accelerate
some of the algorithms

00:25:37.422 --> 00:25:39.380
people are deploying for
traditional predictive

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:40.340
analytics.

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:43.640
Particularly, gradient boosting
decision trees, or XGBoost,

00:25:43.640 --> 00:25:46.340
comes up a lot in
forecasting, wanting

00:25:46.340 --> 00:25:49.400
to know when I'm going to
go out of stock of bananas

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:50.760
and I need to ship more bananas.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:53.510
There's point-of-sale data
for every retail store

00:25:53.510 --> 00:25:55.370
all around the world.

00:25:55.370 --> 00:25:58.040
And data scientists
are drowning in data.

00:25:58.040 --> 00:25:59.837
They just lack the
compute to actually make

00:25:59.837 --> 00:26:02.170
those kinds of predictions
and often in the time windows

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:05.000
that matter, which can
be literally just hours.

00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:08.000
So we're starting to see
much more acceleration

00:26:08.000 --> 00:26:10.170
of traditional machine learning.

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:12.260
We have a software
stack called RAPIDS,

00:26:12.260 --> 00:26:14.840
which is bringing some of
those algorithms-- collection

00:26:14.840 --> 00:26:16.937
of algorithms-- and
GPU-ifying them,

00:26:16.937 --> 00:26:18.770
making them available
to people to integrate

00:26:18.770 --> 00:26:20.717
into their predictive
analytics platforms.

00:26:20.717 --> 00:26:22.550
Particularly, we've
been working with Google

00:26:22.550 --> 00:26:24.230
on accelerating those as well.

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:25.855
And that's some of
the stuff that we've

00:26:25.855 --> 00:26:27.380
been talking about here at GTC.

00:26:27.380 --> 00:26:31.640
And it's exciting to see not
just deep learning but all

00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:34.460
of machine learning recognize
the opportunity that GPUs

00:26:34.460 --> 00:26:36.320
can bring to accelerating
all those kinds

00:26:36.320 --> 00:26:37.430
of predictive workloads.

