the easiest place
is NGC.NVIDIA.com.

00:26:51.800 --> 00:26:53.600
We have a lot of containers.

00:26:53.600 --> 00:26:54.660
We have examples.

00:26:54.660 --> 00:26:57.770
We have models and scripts
of exactly the recipes

00:26:57.770 --> 00:26:59.270
that we have applied
to get the best

00:26:59.270 --> 00:27:02.000
performance to the state
of the art accuracy for all

00:27:02.000 --> 00:27:03.110
of these models.

00:27:03.110 --> 00:27:04.970
Like Ian said before,
we work really

00:27:04.970 --> 00:27:07.760
hard to put this into
open-source software

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:08.750
as much as possible.

00:27:08.750 --> 00:27:12.070
So if you're using a
GCP VM, we actually

00:27:12.070 --> 00:27:13.977
stay in contact with
the VM team to make sure

00:27:13.977 --> 00:27:16.310
that they're getting the
latest optimizations that we've

00:27:16.310 --> 00:27:18.830
contributed to TensorFlow so
that they're working perfectly

00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:20.630
on our latest GPUs.

00:27:20.630 --> 00:27:23.570
IAN BUCK: We've also been
providing educational services

00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:27.080
as well. so our GTC conference,
we host many hackathons.

00:27:27.080 --> 00:27:28.820
We host many tutorials.

00:27:28.820 --> 00:27:32.125
We have our own Deep Learning
Institute, which we call DLI.

00:27:32.125 --> 00:27:33.500
Frequently,
customers come to us,

00:27:33.500 --> 00:27:35.330
and they want to
just learn about AI.

00:27:35.330 --> 00:27:39.500
So we have a program where
anyone can come and sign up

00:27:39.500 --> 00:27:41.875
and we'll run a Deep Learning
Institute at their company.

00:27:41.875 --> 00:27:43.917
We'll show them how to
use the latest containers.

00:27:43.917 --> 00:27:46.970
We'll show them how to configure
their GPUs to get them started.

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.500
They'll actually train a
neural network, go use it.

00:27:49.500 --> 00:27:52.353
And by doing the canonical
use case of a toy use case,

00:27:52.353 --> 00:27:54.770
they can then turn around and
go back into their companies

00:27:54.770 --> 00:27:57.230
and go from being just
a DevOps person or just

00:27:57.230 --> 00:27:59.613
a traditional data scientist
to being an AI expert

00:27:59.613 --> 00:28:02.030
and figure out how to apply
these things to their business

00:28:02.030 --> 00:28:02.963
use cases.

00:28:02.963 --> 00:28:04.880
We're very active in
that community of people,

00:28:04.880 --> 00:28:06.290
obviously working with
many customers that

00:28:06.290 --> 00:28:08.840
want to get started on GPUs,
want to get started in AI,

00:28:08.840 --> 00:28:10.310
just don't know how.

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:12.358
We see it our mission to
help educate and help.

00:28:12.358 --> 00:28:14.150
KARI BRISKI: And I know
we said it earlier,

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:15.680
but the product
manager in me really

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:17.430
cares about the end
user and their persona

00:28:17.430 --> 00:28:19.305
and what they're trying
to do with their job.

00:28:19.305 --> 00:28:21.140
And so if you're a
system administrator,

00:28:21.140 --> 00:28:23.815
you can feel good about our
contributions to Kubernetes

00:28:23.815 --> 00:28:25.940
and check out our [INAUDIBLE]
center GPU management

00:28:25.940 --> 00:28:26.880
utilities.

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:29.180
And again, all of this
is on NGC.NVIDIA.com.

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:32.120
If you're a deep
learning practitioner,

00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:33.860
definitely check out AMP.

00:28:33.860 --> 00:28:36.080
Again, that's in the
framework that you use most

00:28:36.080 --> 00:28:38.580
to take advantage of
mixed precision training.

00:28:38.580 --> 00:28:40.580
We've also made available
Kubeflow Pipelines, so

00:28:40.580 --> 00:28:43.730
examples directly into the
Kubeflow Pipeline repository

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:45.530
across a variety
of applications.

00:28:45.530 --> 00:28:47.280
We're entering one
for medical imaging.

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:49.970
We're entering one for
intelligent video analytics,

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:50.957
for transfer learning.

00:28:50.957 --> 00:28:53.540
And then we're also doing, like,
the "Hello world" with ResNet

00:28:53.540 --> 00:28:56.480
50 and deploying that on an
inference server with Kubeflow

00:28:56.480 --> 00:28:57.680
Pipelines into Kubernetes.

00:28:57.680 --> 00:28:59.388
And then if you're
the software developer

00:28:59.388 --> 00:29:02.130
who really doesn't know anything
about deep learning models--

00:29:02.130 --> 00:29:04.463
you just know that you have
to put it into your software

00:29:04.463 --> 00:29:07.487
application to get this, you
know, say, yeah, I'm using AI--

00:29:07.487 --> 00:29:09.320
definitely check our
inference server that's

00:29:09.320 --> 00:29:10.627
also open-source software.

00:29:10.627 --> 00:29:12.710
MARK MANDEL: One of my
favorite questions to ask--

00:29:12.710 --> 00:29:15.293
what's been the most interesting
or potentially weird or wacky

00:29:15.293 --> 00:29:18.267
thing that you've seen people
do with AI or NVIDIA chips?

00:29:18.267 --> 00:29:19.850
KARI BRISKI: Well,
it's kind of wacky,

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:22.100
but one thing that's
gotten a lot of notice

00:29:22.100 --> 00:29:23.932
lately is the celebrity
face synthesis.

00:29:23.932 --> 00:29:25.640
It's gotten a lot of
press and attention.

00:29:25.640 --> 00:29:28.520
And I think there is even a site
that you can click on and try

00:29:28.520 --> 00:29:30.860
and guess which face is real.

00:29:30.860 --> 00:29:32.588
That's been kind of
the neat one lately.

00:29:32.588 --> 00:29:34.130
IAN BUCK: I've seen
my face plastered

00:29:34.130 --> 00:29:37.730
on a few people, which is
very interesting to watch.

00:29:37.730 --> 00:29:38.995
MARK MANDEL: It's fine.

00:29:38.995 --> 00:29:41.120
IAN BUCK: There are all
sorts of applications of AI

00:29:41.120 --> 00:29:42.050
that I've seen.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:44.850
I think some of the coolest ones
are actually in agriculture.

00:29:44.850 --> 00:29:45.160
MARK MANDEL: Oh, interesting.

00:29:45.160 --> 00:29:46.790
IAN BUCK: People don't
think about it that much.

00:29:46.790 --> 00:29:49.100
But I believe there's a
cow with a Fitbit on it,

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:50.683
and they're trying
to figure out the--

00:29:50.683 --> 00:29:52.142
MARK MANDEL: Getting
your steps in?

00:29:52.142 --> 00:29:54.590
IAN BUCK: Yeah, trying to
figure out the health of cows,

00:29:54.590 --> 00:29:57.710
improve milk output,
understand when cows are sick,

00:29:57.710 --> 00:30:00.470
using AI to understand herds--

00:30:00.470 --> 00:30:01.470
very cool stuff.

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:04.543
There's actually interesting
work in strawberry picking.

00:30:04.543 --> 00:30:06.710
I didn't know this, but
apparently strawberry fields

00:30:06.710 --> 00:30:08.720
need to be picked
every three days.

00:30:08.720 --> 00:30:10.688
So it's not a crop
you grow and pick it.

00:30:10.688 --> 00:30:12.230
You have to constantly
be picking it,

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:15.170
and it's an area that is
actually very labor-intensive.

00:30:15.170 --> 00:30:17.493
And obviously, it's hard
to find all this farm

00:30:17.493 --> 00:30:19.910
labor that can constantly come
and pick it for three days.

00:30:19.910 --> 00:30:22.550
So there's all this cool
stuff in robotics right now.

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:24.170
There's a strawberry
picker that's

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:27.560
being developed with GPUs
that has a little articulating

00:30:27.560 --> 00:30:28.558
arm and a camera.

00:30:28.558 --> 00:30:30.350
And so it's actually
a really hard problem.

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:32.750
You've got to figure out
and do segment analysis.

00:30:32.750 --> 00:30:34.755
Which pixels are
which strawberries,

00:30:34.755 --> 00:30:35.630
even if they overlap?

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:38.232
And what's the ideal place
to grab the stem and cut it?

00:30:38.232 --> 00:30:40.190
You never want to actually
touch the strawberry

00:30:40.190 --> 00:30:42.862
because that would actually
potentially transfer

00:30:42.862 --> 00:30:44.570
funguses or molds and
that kind of stuff.

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:45.620
MARK MANDEL: And you don't
want to squish strawberries.

00:30:45.620 --> 00:30:47.090
IAN BUCK: You don't
want to squish them,

00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:48.170
and you've got to do
it every three days.

00:30:48.170 --> 00:30:50.503
So there's some really cool
stuff happening in robotics.

00:30:50.503 --> 00:30:52.948
And that, I think, is some
of the next-generation stuff

00:30:52.948 --> 00:30:54.740
that we're going to
start seeing coming out

00:30:54.740 --> 00:30:57.200
of AI, just very convincing,
very compelling, very

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:59.830
interesting robotics
applications that are going

00:30:59.830 --> 00:31:01.460
to make our lives a lot easier.

00:31:01.460 --> 00:31:03.800
JON FOUST: Being a
gamer, I do happen

00:31:03.800 --> 00:31:06.440
to have RTX 2080--
just curious to see

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:08.450
if we can see that
same technology that

00:31:08.450 --> 00:31:11.210
is being introduced in RTX
within the GPUs that are going

00:31:11.210 --> 00:31:12.260
to be in data centers?

00:31:12.260 --> 00:31:14.052
IAN BUCK: Yeah, one
thing we've always done

00:31:14.052 --> 00:31:16.790
is we made our technology
available on all of our GPUs.

00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:18.710
And that's made us so
successful is the fact

00:31:18.710 --> 00:31:21.500
that anyone can get access
to the latest NVIDIA tech,

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:22.700
even if they're a gamer.

00:31:22.700 --> 00:31:24.770
Because many of the
people actually inventing

00:31:24.770 --> 00:31:29.260
the next-generation stuff,
whether it be science or AI,

00:31:29.260 --> 00:31:30.920
they're probably gamers too.

00:31:30.920 --> 00:31:34.550
So with RTX actually, all
the Tensor Core capabilities

00:31:34.550 --> 00:31:35.807
are there, available.

00:31:35.807 --> 00:31:37.640
We're already starting
to put it into games.

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:40.310
And in fact, there's work
being done for a final frame

00:31:40.310 --> 00:31:41.160
rendering.

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:43.250
So the very end of a
frame-- of a rendering

00:31:43.250 --> 00:31:45.800
of a frame of a game-- we
actually can apply AI to it

00:31:45.800 --> 00:31:47.570
to improve its visual quality.

00:31:47.570 --> 00:31:50.120
We actually spent a lot of work
training the neural network

00:31:50.120 --> 00:31:52.230
to improve the image quality.

00:31:52.230 --> 00:31:54.710
We actually render it in
much higher resolution

00:31:54.710 --> 00:31:57.320
and then downscale
and teach an AI

00:31:57.320 --> 00:32:00.380
how to do automatic
color correction,

00:32:00.380 --> 00:32:02.690
to eliminate jaggies,
to do anti-aliasing

00:32:02.690 --> 00:32:04.840
using AI on every frame.

00:32:04.840 --> 00:32:08.300
That's like millions of
pixels 60 times a second.

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:09.800
We actually do a
full neural network

00:32:09.800 --> 00:32:11.800
on every one of those
pixels to actually improve

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:15.620
it to do a final pass for
AA improvement on your GPU.

00:32:15.620 --> 00:32:17.060
It's a really hard thing.

00:32:17.060 --> 00:32:18.490
I mean, that's real real-time.

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:20.060
JON FOUST: I could imagine.

00:32:20.060 --> 00:32:22.940
IAN BUCK: That's why I think AI
is going to affect and impact

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:24.800
and improve every one
of the applications

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:28.190
with GPUs in every industry,
not just agriculture

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:32.210
but gaming, automation,
robotics, the cloud.

00:32:32.210 --> 00:32:33.463
It's super exciting.

